## Vercel Web Analytics Implementation

Successfully installed and configured Vercel Web Analytics for this Next.js portfolio project.

### Changes Made

**1. Package Installation**
- Installed `@vercel/analytics` version 2.0.1
- Updated `package.json` to include the new dependency
- Updated `package-lock.json` with the new package and its dependencies

**2. Analytics Integration**
Modified: `src/app/[lang]/layout.tsx`
- Added import statement: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next'`
- Added `<Analytics />` component in the body section, next to the existing `<SpeedInsights />` component

### Implementation Details

Following the official Vercel Analytics documentation (https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart), the Analytics component was added to the root layout file, which is the recommended approach for Next.js App Router applications.

The implementation follows the existing pattern in the codebase:
- Placed alongside the existing `@vercel/speed-insights` integration
- Uses the Next.js-specific import path (`@vercel/analytics/next`)
- Added as a sibling component to `<SpeedInsights />` at the end of the body tag

### Testing & Verification

✅ Build completed successfully (`npm run build`)
✅ TypeScript compilation passed (existing type errors unrelated to changes)
✅ ESLint passed (existing warning unrelated to changes)
✅ All 14 pages generated successfully as static/SSG routes

### Next Steps

After deployment to Vercel:
1. Enable Web Analytics in the Vercel dashboard (Analytics section)
2. Verify tracking by checking browser Network tab for analytics requests
3. Analytics data will appear in the dashboard within days after user visits

### Files Modified

- `andreas-technology-v3/package.json` - Added @vercel/analytics dependency
- `andreas-technology-v3/package-lock.json` - Updated with new dependency tree
- `andreas-technology-v3/src/app/[lang]/layout.tsx` - Added Analytics component import and usage
